{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "The query explicitly asks to break resolution time down by agent, so an agent axis is essential. However, the original table already contains 'assigned_to' and 'closed_by', making this column largely a redundant restatement of existing agent information. Values appear soundly grounded in the source (agent names present in the data), but the redundancy sharply limits its added analytical leverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "This is a strong new grouping axis directly required by the 'by issue type' half of the query. Although an original 'category' column exists, this column derives a normalized issue type from the free-text short_description, and the samples show sound categorization (e.g., 'Unable to access the database' -> database_access, 'VPN issue' -> vpn_connectivity). Genuine leverage for grouping resolution time."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_closure_match",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.15,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A near-constant boolean (478 true / 22 false) comparing sys_updated_on to closed_at. It offers no axis on agent or issue type and does not help measure resolution time variation, so it adds little analytical leverage for this query. Values are derivable directly from the two timestamp columns and appear correct in the samples where the timestamps are identical."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
  "source_groundedness": 0.88,
  "rationale": "The set contains one clearly valuable derived column (issue_category) that directly supports the 'by issue type' grouping, one redundant agent column duplicating existing fields, and one near-constant boolean of little relevance to resolution-time analysis. Groundedness is generally high: produced values are sound readings of the source text and timestamps, with no observed hallucination. Overall usefulness is moderate, dragged down by redundancy and the trivial boolean flag."
}

